WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.929
CSUN Professor Eric Edson: These comments
that flow into things…actually it was one

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:05.580
of your clips.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.730
Somebody down there was saying “I hate plot!

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.240
I want story!”

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.840
And that poor person is obviously not educated
about these things because plot and story

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.640
are the same bloody thing.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:21.840
Story is plot.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.500
Plot is story.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.179
Maybe we can get there in a minute or two.

00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:31.880
But make those mistakes and that’s just
ignorance.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:37.390
You can get educated and you can learn the
tools and how to use them.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:41.750
To be out there and say “I’m going to
create this beautiful, big, intricate thing”

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:47.560
and not know the pieces is like saying “I’m
going to create this big, beautiful house

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:55.880
and not know anything about cement, electrical
cabling, roofing shingles, glass.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:59.670
You’ve got to know all these things before
you can build a house.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:05.080
And I believe the same is true for really
good screenwriting.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:12.410
I’ll throw out some of the silly stuff but
useful stuff.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:19.410
Did you know that there are only four variable
goals in all narrative screenwriting?

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:26.170
Win, Stop, Escape or Retrieve.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:31.880
So okay, you’ve got an idea for a character
or hero or heroine, great!

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.140
And they have…okay now what is it they want
or they have to have a goal to pursue.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.810
Well it has to fall into the category of Win,
Stop, Escape or Retrieve.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.550
Now these are big categories.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:46.450
There are many versions of each one.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:52.390
Many permutations of each one, so it’s not
like it’s that cut and dried but it will

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.960
appear to a lot of people like “That’s
too cut and dried for me!”

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:02.780
The point is this there is only those four
because those four share something in common.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:14.319
They all provide a visible, physical, three-dimensional
goal for motion picture that will result in

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:20.420
a physical climax and obligatory scene.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.880
It preclude the use of internal goals and
that’s one of the things people have to

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:27.990
get over.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:32.890
An internal goal, that is great and it’s
separate from what drives the movie.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.200
You need a physical, visible goal.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.280
Each one of those four goals has a finish
line, a visible finish line and a goal like…well…they

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.319
want to feel better about themselves.

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:48.440
That doesn’t work on film, it just doesn’t
work.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.520
And that there are only four emotions.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.360
Mad, Glad, Sad and Scared.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.770
You haven’t heard that one?

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.470
Film Courage: No?

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:06.069
Eric Edson: Again, there is a lot of variation
between Mad, Sad, Glad and Scared there are

00:03:06.069 --> 00:03:13.599
many permutations and dimensions there but
the way they are useful is like this: if you

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:19.490
are building, if you are outlining something
a section (a particular section) Hero-Goal

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:26.020
Sequence or a particular scene the thing is
the way you guarantee that it’s going to

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:32.260
have conflict and change in it…change is
one of the single most important…we can

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.820
talk about that in a minute or two.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:41.260
Change is THE power that makes screenplays
either work or not work (you’ve got to quantify

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:48.420
it but it can be done wonderfully) but that
if the hero (lead character) walks, stomps,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:54.959
jumps, falls into a scene and they are either
Mad, Sad, Glad or Scared by the END of the

00:03:54.959 --> 00:03:58.680
scene, they have to be one of the others.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.680
If they are mad when they show up, they have
to be scared when they walk away.

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.349
Or if they are scared when they show up, they
have to be glad when they walk away because

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:14.400
that guarantees dramatic change in a scene
and it’s a way of checking yourself because

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.889
there are a whole lot of scenes that are just
talk and they are the same and everything

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:22.710
is the same at the end as it was in the beginning.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:29.229
And that is not a viable scene for visual
storytelling (things like that).

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:34.830
Do you know there are only 14 character categories
in all of dramatic writing and all of narrative

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.590
writing?

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:44.589
And you have to know how they work and how
they interrelate, the puzzle pieces to relate,

00:04:44.589 --> 00:04:51.129
plot, how they advance, how they can advance
the story…

00:04:51.129 --> 00:04:54.130
Film Courage: What are a few of the 14?

00:04:54.130 --> 00:05:01.240
Eric Edson: For instance…let me qualify
them…there are 14 character categories that

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.150
advance and serve story.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:15.280
Story is a central character who undertakes
to do something (psychically do something)

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:20.419
in order to achieve a psychical, visible goal
by the end.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:28.080
And in that pursuit there are 14 other character
categories that serve as either helpers or

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.050
hinderers toward that hero.

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:38.749
First of all the main categories are either
hero or adversary.

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:41.999
Adversary is much unsung and under-appreciated.

00:05:41.999 --> 00:05:46.479
The advisory is critical.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.360
Without conflict you don’t have story.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.800
There is no story without conflict.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.770
That’s just it.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:56.999
So that adversary is key for you to get conflict
and that puts a lot of weight on the adversary.

00:05:56.999 --> 00:06:06.849
Hero, adversary, then there is mentor (a very
wide and delicious category).

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:12.699
Back to Joe Campbell and the assessment of
mythological approach.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:15.659
There are some of those character.

00:06:15.659 --> 00:06:17.240
I mean he called the adversary a shapeshifter.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.089
That’s the more specific use, yes it’s
adversary.

00:06:22.089 --> 00:06:26.979
Shapeshifter is something that appears one
thing on the outside and becomes something

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:27.979
else.

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:31.599
That is one kind of adversary (but not the
only kind).

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:34.789
And then there is mentor, there is the love
interest character.

00:06:34.789 --> 00:06:43.130
This is interesting…I really go into this
in detail for folks, for some…the love interest

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:54.219
character brings depth and humanity and helps,
augments, eases character growth forward for

00:06:54.219 --> 00:06:56.330
the central character.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:01.660
And it is the least used character category
of all the grad students who pass through

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:02.660
here.

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:03.660
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.129
Eric Edson: Yeah, it really is.

00:07:05.129 --> 00:07:09.499
And I think it’s the character…it gets
too close to home.

00:07:09.499 --> 00:07:16.719
Maybe their experience in these things, emotional
central experiences are highly limited or

00:07:16.719 --> 00:07:17.860
something?

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:18.860
What it is?

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:24.289
Something about it but that is a key character
in stories that work and you have a romance

00:07:24.289 --> 00:07:26.319
subplot that love interest character.

00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:30.960
Whoah…and it’s done well, it’s one of
the most powerful things you can add to a

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:31.960
story.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.139
And then there are helper/follower allies.

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:37.620
And sidekick.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.360
Sidekick is another category.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.999
Very specific function displayed by the sidekick
and others.

00:07:44.999 --> 00:07:47.370
There is the adversary agent.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.189
See there is one adversary.

00:07:49.189 --> 00:07:50.750
Adversary can only be one person.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:57.889
A human being or a personified thing, a monster
that behaves like a human being and thinks

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:02.159
like a human being, but one thing, one human
being.

00:08:02.159 --> 00:08:08.990
But they can also have all kinds of adversary
agents running around doing the bidding of

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:09.990
the adversary.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:15.580
Film Courage: So let’s say in ERIN BROCKOVICH,
they are fighting this toxic tort or whatever.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:25.869
And so the workers of the plant are sort of
the adversary agents because they are not

00:08:25.869 --> 00:08:29.050
totally calling the shots but they are working
for the people calling the shots.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.050
Eric Edson: Yes.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.020
Except for the ones that turn to the good
side, you know?

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.800
Who jump over to the good people.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:44.280
And then there’s a group of characters called
gate guardians and I would put those people

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:46.620
in the gate guardian category.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:53.560
Gate guardian is a character (this is from
Campbell and the mythological structure, too).

00:08:53.560 --> 00:09:01.340
Gate guardian is a character who when first
met by the hero, first confronted by the hero,

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.300
stops them, says “No!

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.570
You cannot enter here.”

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:12.960
But once the hero finds a way to outsmart
that gate guardian, it’s never brute force.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.630
It’s when they outsmart them, find a way
to get around and continue their journey after

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:23.580
that the gate guardian becomes an ally.

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:26.510
At first opposes and then assists.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:30.200
And there are a few more in there, too.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:35.750
But the point of this is…all of these characters…there
are only 14 that help you create a plot.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:40.890
That help move a story forward and any other
character that the particular writer wants

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.220
to plot…”I really like this character.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.330
They’re kind of interesting and peppy and
zippy.

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:47.750
And they talk funny and I like it.”

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:54.320
If they do not serve the story, if they do
not help drive the plot forward…[whispers]

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.870
get rid of them!

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:01.150
Loose them because they have to serve a function.

